AB  - Crop diseases significantly impact global food security and agricultural productivity. Accurate and timely detection of crop diseases is vital for effective interventions. However, traditional diagnostic methods often fall short due to subjectivity and reliance on human expertise. Addressing this challenge, this study proposes a hybrid Deep Learning (DL) approach for crop disease classification. A novel Dirichlet Weighted Ensemble (DWE) model was developed, assigning adaptive weights to individual models using F1-score-driven Dirichlet priors. The ensemble included Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) optimized using Optuna and validated with 5-fold cross-validation. The model was trained on a curated dataset compiled from five public data sources, comprising 56,044 images from 36 disease classes across 16 crop species, integrating real-field and lab-controlled environments. The best-performing weighted ensemble—comprising InceptionV3, Xception, and the mobile variant of the Neural Architecture Search Network (NASNetMobile)—achieved an accuracy, weighted F1-score, precision, and recall of 99.52 %, and a Receiver Operating Characteristic Area Under Curve (ROC AUC) of 1.000. External validation on unseen datasets confirmed the model's generalizability. An ablation study established its effectiveness over conventional ensemble methods. Deployment feasibility was evaluated through inference time, model size, and memory usage, indicating suitability for web, mobile, and Internet of Things (IoT) platforms. Furthermore, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) were applied to a stacking meta-ensemble using Categorical Boosting (CatBoost) and qualitatively validated by a domain expert. This hybrid approach bridges advanced Artificial Intelligence (AI) techniques with agricultural engineering, enabling timely, data-driven interventions for sustainable crop health management.
