AB  - Precisely identifying moving loads on structures is a cornerstone of structural health monitoring (SHM), providing critical diagnostic insights by linking external forces to observed dynamic responses to ensure structural safety. To overcome the challenges of achieving high precision and the need for extensive sensor deployment in moving load identification on structures such as bridges or ship decks, an Attention mechanism-based Multi-modal Fusion and Bidirectional Gated Recurrent Unit (AMF-BiGRU) moving load identification model is proposed in this paper. Features from displacement and acceleration responses are extracted using Bidirectional Gated Recurrent Unit (BiGRU) layers. An attention-based mechanism then performs feature-level fusion, adaptively weighting structural response contributions to facilitate complementary information sharing crucial for accurate load identification. Additionally, results demonstrate accurate reconstruction and localization by using two measurement points. Across diverse test cases involving varying vehicle weights, speeds, and alternating load forms, the relative percentage errors (RPE) for reconstruction and localization were consistently low, remaining below 0.45 % and 0.94 %, respectively. Compared to baseline fusion strategies and TimesNet, the AMF-BiGRU model demonstrated superior performance. The model also exhibited strong robustness, maintaining high accuracy under noise levels up to 10 %.
