AB  - Community detection, a significant research topic in network analysis, aims to identify multiple tightly connected groups of nodes. While contrastive learning has attracted considerable attention for its outstanding task performance, research on more reliable positive and negative sample pairs is lacking. Furthermore, imbalance between these pairs significantly impacts model performance. To address these limitations, we present a new community detection method named Dual Contrastive Learning with Auxiliary Supervision (DCL-AS). Initially, we propose a comprehensive strategy for mining reliable hard samples: instead of random selection, we combine community importance scores and connection importance scores to identify more representative and reliable samples, laying a robust foundation for model training. Subsequently, we develop a dual contrastive loss that integrates the reliable hard sample pairs contrastive loss and redundancy reduction contrastive loss, which effectively facilitates node differentiation and alleviates the appearance of sample imbalance. Additionally, we design an auxiliary supervision module that guides the backbone network via a graph attention network-based guidance network. The guidance network leverages the graph’s higher-order modularity (a measure of community structure robustness) to preserve complex community information and improve detection accuracy. Experimental results on six publicly available datasets demonstrate that the method outperforms state-of-the-art algorithms. The corresponding code is available at https://github.com/wuqiqi537/DCL-AS.
