AB  - The bearing data generation model can improve the performance of intelligent bearing fault diagnosis models under data scarcity and class imbalance by expanding the original data. However, existing models typically rely solely on a single feature domain or a combination of the time and frequency domains to constrain the sample generation process. As a result, the generated samples cannot accurately represent the actual characteristics of real data in the constrained feature domains and exhibit significant differences from real data in the unconstrained ones, and their diversity is also limited. Therefore, a Multi-Domain Collaborative Bearing Data Generation Model (MDC-BDG) is proposed. First, a Multi-Domain Collaborative Constraint Mechanism (MDCCM) is designed to make the feature distributions of the samples generated by MDC-BDG simultaneously resemble real data in the time, frequency, and time-frequency domains, and enhance their diversity. Second, a Cross-Level Interaction Temporal module (CLIT) is constructed to further enhance the performance of generated samples in all feature domains. Finally, the experimental results on two practical cases show that the average Dynamic Time Warping, Cosine similarity, and Structural Similarity Index between the samples generated by MDC-BDG and the real data are 96.6928 and 246.9739, 0.8726 and 0.8283, 0.9577 and 0.9549, respectively. Furthermore, MDC-BDG can assist the downstream fault diagnosis model in achieving accuracies of 97.22 % and 95.63 % with only 20 data per fault category, respectively. These indicators surpass cutting-edge bearing data generation models, effectively demonstrating that the samples generated by MDC-BDG have superior comprehensive quality, diversity, and practical application value.
