AB  - This research presents a numerical investigation on the flame structure and energy-exergy-environmental-economic analysis in the context of moderate to intense low-oxygen dilution combustion with optimization through an artificial neural network and the Non-Dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm II. To simulate the turbulent flow phenomenon, the employed methodology involved the utilization of the Reynolds-averaged Navier-Stokes technique along with the Reynolds-Normalization Group k-ε turbulence model. Concurrently, the turbulence-combustion interaction was represented through the eddy dissipation concept model in combination with the GRI-Mech 2.11 chemical reaction mechanism. Moderate to intense low-oxygen dilution combustion characteristics, such as exergy efficiency, combustion efficiency, fuel consumption, and emissions, were investigated at different distances from the fuel inlet nozzle by varying the fuel jet Reynolds number, preheater temperature, and burner inlet oxygen concentration. The system was modeled using an artificial neural network, determining parameters via Pearson's correlation to optimize the system. Then, the optimal points were identified, and the results were compared to the numerical outputs. The optimized model was found to have high accuracy despite small errors. The optimal fuel jet velocity, preheater temperature, and preheater oxygen concentration were 70 m/s, 1300 K, and 5 %, respectively. Finally, the energy-exergy-environmental-economic analysis demonstrated significant improvements, with NOx emissions reduced by 84%, CO emissions by 57.6%, and methane consumption by 34.2%, while only minor changes occurred in combustion and exergy efficiencies.
