AB  - Understanding how opinions are expressed and exchanged is critical for numerous applications, such as opinion management and policy development. Simulation-based methods have been widely used to enhance this understanding in the context of traditional media. However, existing methods fail to capture the patterns of people's interactions with large language models (LLMs) and the consequent dramatic impacts of LLMs on opinion dynamics. To address these, we develop a novel opinion network dynamics model to encode the outputs of LLMs and interaction patterns between LLMs and individuals. Using the proposed model, we conduct millions of simulations to investigate the impact of LLMs on opinion dynamics in a variety of scenarios. The results suggested that the output opinion of LLMs has a unique and positive effect on the collective opinion difference. There is 38.6 % more opinion diversity when people all partially rely on LLMs, compared to prohibiting the use of LLMs entirely. Our simulations also indicate that by introducing extra agents with opposite/neutral/random opinions, we can effectively mitigate the impact of negative output from LLMs. Our methodology and findings provide valuable insights into opinion dynamics in the era of LLMs, although some limitations remain since realistic opinion interactions are far more complex.
