AB  - Images captured in hazy environments frequently exhibit degraded visibility, loss of structural detail, and color distortion. These degradations impair both human perception and downstream computer vision applications. While deep learning-based dehazing methods have made considerable progress, current approaches — whether based on Convolutional Neural Networks or Transformers — often fail to adequately model both long-range haze distributions and fine local textures, due to limitations in receptive field size or computational complexity. Furthermore, these methods fail to explore the complementary advantages of multi-frequency representations within a unified framework and lack integration with state-space modeling. This shortcoming makes it challenging to restore both global structures and local details simultaneously. To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel dehazing framework that integrates multi-frequency feature fusion with selective state-space modeling. The core of our framework is a Dual-Frequency Enhancement Module, which collaboratively leverages Fourier transforms for global spectral modeling and Wavelet transforms for local detail recovery. In addition, we design an Omnidirectional Symmetric Spectrum Scanning Mechanism to capture directional frequency dependencies and a Context-Detail Fusion Module to semantically align encoder–decoder features. Experiments on multiple synthetic and real-world datasets demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms state-of-the-art approaches in both subjective visual quality and objective metrics. Specifically, on the Non-Homogeneous Haze and the Dense Haze datasets, the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio was improved by 2.80 decibels and 2.55 decibels, respectively, compared to the baseline algorithms. Experimental results demonstrate that our framework effectively unifies global clarity and detail recovery, significantly enhancing dehazing performance under complex haze conditions.
