AB  - Terrestrial water storage changes derived from the Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE)/GRACE Follow-On (GFO) have become important scientific data in hydrology and related fields, but its coarse spatial resolution has greatly hindered their further application. Deep learning algorithms have been widely used in GRACE/GFO spatial downscaling. However, the geographical spatial heterogeneity and nonlinear changes have adverse impacts on the construction of high-precision downscaling algorithm. Thus, we innovatively proposed a partitioned long short-term memory network combined with extreme value correction (EV-PLSTM) algorithm to improve the spatial downscaling accuracy of GRACE/GFO data from 1° to 0.1° in this study, and evaluated the reliability and improvement effect of the algorithm through closed-loop verification. The results show that compared with gradient boosting (GB) and random forest (RF), EV-PLSTM reduces the root mean square error (RMSE) by 47.51 % and 71.55 %, respectively, and the mean absolute error (MAE) by 54.24 % and 75.35 %, respectively. Taking the Yangtze River Basin (YRB) as an example, our downscaling results revealed the fine-scale spatial variations in regional terrestrial water storage anomalies (TWSA) under the influence of strong El Niño-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) events. The positive TWSA mainly concentrated in the southeastern region of the YRB and a delayed response of approximately seven months in comparison to the ENSO signal. This study provides a useful reference for the application of artificial intelligence in the field of satellite gravity hydrology.
