KW  - Expensive problems
KW  - Autoencoder
KW  - Knowledge transfer
KW  - Offspring expansion
KW  - Radial basis function
KW  - Cross-space search
AB  - Surrogate-assisted evolutionary algorithms face significant challenges as problem scale and complexity increase in generating promising solutions. Typically, high-dimensional optimization is mapped to a reduced, low-dimensional subspace for processing. However, these methods often distort search space topology and lose feature information due to dimensionality reduction. To address this, this paper introduces a cross-space collaborative differential evolution method with knowledge transfer and dual-offspring expansion for high-dimensional expensive problems, called CSCDE. CSCDE employs an autoencoder to encode the high-dimensional space into a low-dimensional subspace. The optimal topological knowledge extracted from solutions in this subspace is iteratively decoded and transferred back to the original space. A radial basis function model approximates both the high-dimensional original space and its subspace, reducing computational costs and speeding up the search for promising regions through dual-space exploration. To maintain diversity and ensure convergence, a dual-offspring expansion increases candidate versatility each iteration. Additionally, a customized opposition-learning strategy diversifies the parent population, while a roulette-guided directional mutation creates offspring solutions. Compared with nine advanced surrogate-assisted evolutionary optimizers, CSCDE obtains the best results on at least 19 out of 29 problems from the IEEE Congress on Evolutionary Computation 2017 benchmark suite, achieving performance improvements of 0.1 %–37 % on an open shared economic dispatch problem and 15.5 %–97 % on a real-world high-dimensional power system optimization problem, demonstrating its superior convergence and efficiency.
