AB  - The unexpected outbreak of infectious diseases and changes in healthcare practices have severely hastened medical waste production, which poses significant consequences for environmental health and ecology. The hazardous nature of medical waste and its high risk and cost of misclassification demand intelligent, green, and risk-sensitive waste management systems. This study first offers an incremental and adaptive learning-based Waste Classification System (WCS) for medical waste supply chain management. WCS combines pre-trained convolutional neural network models, Unified Adaptive Cost-Sensitive Learning for managing asymmetric misclassification costs, and Active Incremental Fine-Tuning to enhance long-term classification accuracy. Second, a multi-objective mathematical model is developed to minimize economic costs, environmental impacts, and public health risks. The model optimizes waste to treatment plants, recycling facilities, and landfills based on revised real-time classification. A rolling horizon is applied to dynamically re-synchronize supply chain decisions with varying WCS outputs. The model is demonstrated in a case study in Ahvaz, Iran, in which classification results were iteratively improved over eight iterations. The integrated model provided improved cost-effectiveness and reduced risk, as well as environmental emissions. Sensitivity analysis revealed key operations and managerial implications, ascertaining the system's ability to guide risk-resilient and sustainable medical waste management in dynamic environments.
