KW  - 
KW  - 
KW  - 
KW  - 
AB  - Integrating communication networks in industrial control systems (ICSs) has improved their monitoring and control while intensifying the cybersecurity concerns. This is an important issue in critical infrastructures like power plants (PPs), where sensor data can be manipulated through false data injection attacks (FDIAs). This paper proposes an ensemble learning (EL) model for FDIA detection in PPs, leveraging their nonlinear dynamic model to generate an extensive database for training classifiers. The method applies feature engineering (FE) for data diversification to improve the detection accuracy against attacks with varying sizes and spans. This is specifically crucial for harder-to-detect small-scale and non-simultaneous FDIAs, which are mainly ignored in the existing literature. FE includes a moving average (MA) filter for noise reduction and data derivatives integration for capturing new temporal information. The main novelty lies in the tailored application of EL to the FDIA detection in PPs, aggregating the predictions of different algorithms. Utilizing equal and weighted voting consensus among these simple yet diverse classifiers provides enhanced accuracy with manageable computation time. Localized lightweight EL-based machines are suggested for critical variables instead of the common integrated form. This structure considerably reduces the computation burden, enhancing the practical application for being embedded within the existing security systems. Simulations are implemented on a nuclear PP (NPP) with a realistic load profile and included measurement noise. The results validate the effectiveness of the proposed approach, achieving a superior average detection accuracy of 99.6 % compared to diverse existing methods with average accuracies ranging from 92.9 % to 96.9 %.
