KW  - Secondary controlled hydraulic drive
AB  - In offshore crane-assisted ship-to-ship payload transfer operations under harsh marine conditions, heave compensation functionality is vital for sustaining operational reliability and efficiency. Developed in coordination with a crane-tip anti-swing mechanism, this paper introduces a novel artificial intelligence (AI)-driven control strategy for the secondary regulated active heave compensation (SRAHC) system, wherein both long short-term memory (LSTM) modules for heave motion prediction and physics-informed neural network (PINN) predictors of system dynamics are jointly integrated into the conventional cascade nonlinear model predictive control (NMPC) framework. In contrast to traditional time-series-based prediction approaches, the sliding-window LSTM achieves dependable heave motion forecasting in a data-efficient and assumption-agnostic manner, thereby supporting robust disturbance information provision critical to online rolling optimization. The deployment of the data-driven PINN as a high-fidelity predictive model is motivated by its enhanced physical consistency, improved interpretability, and strong generalization capability, empowered by the physics-guided hybrid learning paradigm, thereby offering a promising pathway to advance control accuracy, ensure closed-loop stability, and preserve the computational tractability of NMPC. Grounded in structured operational phase segmentation and transfer path planning, a thorough comparative analysis conducted within a mechanical-electrical-hydraulic co-simulation platform substantiates the outstanding performance of the proposal in terms of trajectory tracking, heave compensation, practical viability, and operational security.
