KW  - Embedded system
KW  - Energy management
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Adaptive neuro-fuzzy inference system
KW  - Multi-objective optimization
KW  - Three-dimensional network-on-chip
AB  - With the increasing integration density in modern many-core systems, traditional interconnect architectures face significant limitations in scalability, bandwidth, and power efficiency. In particular, three-dimensional (3D) Network on Chips (NoCs) offer enhanced performance and reduced footprint by stacking multiple layers of logic, but they also exacerbate thermal issues due to increased power density and limited heat dissipation paths. Thermal management in 3D NoCs is critical for maintaining performance and reliability in modern System-on-Chips (SoCs), especially for Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS). While thermal-aware routing algorithms can mitigate hotspot formation, their effectiveness depends on accurate and efficient temperature prediction. However, existing Temperature Prediction Algorithms (TPAs) often introduce latency and energy overheads, limiting their practical deployment. In this paper, we propose a lightweight and accurate TPA that integrates Principal Component Analysis (PCA) for input dimensionality reduction and an Adaptive Neuro-Fuzzy Inference System (ANFIS) for temperature prediction. This model is optimized using a multi-objective genetic algorithm to balance accuracy and complexity. The proposed TPA achieves a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 0.0022, with an average inference time of 1.086 μs (μs) and model size under 1.94 kilobyte (KB). The predicted temperatures are then used in a novel thermal-aware routing algorithm that selects paths based on future thermal conditions. Evaluated using the AccessNoxim simulator, the proposed system improves average delay by over 50 % and thermal uniformity by over 31 % compared to state-of-the-art approaches. The results show the potential of predictive, low-overhead routing strategies for enhancing 3D NoC performance under dynamic workloads.
