AB  - Flood and drought risk management are crucial for sustainable resource governance and require an integrated approach that combines policies, strategies, and advanced technologies. This review offers a comprehensive evaluation of both traditional and contemporary methods, including Machine Learning (ML), Neural Networks (NNs), Fuzzy Logic Systems (FLS), and Artificial Intelligence (AI)-based approaches, based on literature from 2013 to 2025. Key sources include research from Elsevier, Springer, the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE), Public Library of Science (PLOS), and Taylor and Francis. The review critically assesses the application of ML techniques, such as Support Vector Machines (SVMs) and Random Forests (RFs), in improving flood prediction and drought assessment. It further explores the use of NNs, including Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, for detailed flood mapping and drought monitoring. Additionally, the role of FLS in managing uncertainties and supporting decision-making processes is examined. A comparison is made between time-series data and spatial image datasets, highlighting their impact on modeling accuracy and application. The paper identifies key challenges, such as the need for methodological generalization, enhanced dataset availability, and the development of effective performance metrics. It suggests future research directions focused on improving data integration, model validation, and computational efficiency to advance the effectiveness of flood and drought risk management strategies.
